QMD-4.0 — Quick Model Derivation (Fourth Generation)
QMD-4.0 is the fourth major refinement of the Quick Model Derivation framework: a fast, deterministic method for converting empirical system behavior into lightweight algebraic models suitable for microcontrollers, embedded control, and high-speed simulation.
QMD-4.0 focuses on three goals:
    1. Zero-dependency derivation
Every stage of QMD-4.0 can be executed without external data, large solvers, or heavy toolchains.
    2. Algebraic equivalence to the physical model
The output model is a compact symbolic form that exactly reproduces the chosen baseline behavior.
    3. Real-time deployability
Each model produced by QMD-4.0 can be compiled into a float-only, branch-free, no-math.h C implementation.

What’s New in 4.0?
1. Unified Algebraic Core
QMD-4.0 replaces all integrators, surrogate lookups, and iterative fitting with a closed-form polynomial efficiency model.
This guarantees:
    • deterministic evaluation
    • microcontroller compatibility
    • reproducible coefficients across Python and C
This is the first QMD version that supports complete equivalence across all languages.

2. State Transition Kernel (STK)
QMD-4.0 introduces the STK, a minimal layer that converts physical inputs
(q, pwm, τ)
into a stable model state and defines how that state transitions under constructive or destructive interference.
STK is the mathematical “bridge” that allows the algebraic DE5 core to participate in higher-order QMD behavior.

3. Constructive / Destructive Symmetry
QMD-4.0 formalizes a long-observed phenomenon first seen in QMD-2.0 test sweeps:
    • Some input combinations amplify system output (“constructive states”)
    • Others attenuate it (“destructive states”)
QMD-4.0 encodes this through a signed gradient rule that is independent of the underlying physical system.
This symmetry model becomes essential in multi-axis or multi-controller systems.

4. Calibration-Ready Architecture
QMD-4.0 includes a fully integrated workflow:
    • generate synthetic baselines
    • produce calibrated coefficients
    • generate C + header
    • generate Python reference
    • run smoke tests
    • verify cross-language equivalence
All with one script and zero external files.

5. Backward Compatibility
While QMD-1.x, 2.x, and 3.x used different forms (lookup tables, mixed derivative layers, surrogate integrators), QMD-4.0 maintains:
    • identical interface
    • identical baseline behavior (after calibration)
    • regression-test compatibility with previous reports
This ensures that modernized QMD-4.0 models seamlessly drop into existing pipelines.

How QMD-4.0 Works (Conceptual Overview)
Step 1 — Select or Generate a Baseline
A baseline describes “the system we want to reproduce.”
QMD-4.0 allows:
    • measured data
    • synthetic data
    • prior QMD outputs (e.g., QMD-3.0)
Step 2 — Algebraic Derivation
The DE5 core polynomial is fitted to the baseline using a constrained, deterministic method that guarantees smooth, monotonic behavior and microcontroller safety.
Output:
A complete set of coefficients producing the desired efficiency curve.
Step 3 — Constructive/Destructive State Assignment
Each point in the domain is labeled according to its signed gradient characteristics.
This enables higher-order reasoning (e.g., stability analysis, interference prediction).
Step 4 — Model Export
QMD-4.0 generates:
    • *.c and *.h microcontroller files
    • a pure-Python mirror
    • smoke-test verification and regression tests
    • optional CSV manifests
All artifacts are guaranteed consistent.

Why QMD-4.0 Exists
Traditional empirical modeling workflows require:
    • large datasets
    • nonlinear solvers
    • numerical integration
    • platform-specific code
QMD-4.0 removes all of that.
The entire system can be regenerated from scratch in seconds, produces deterministic algebraic models, and supports deployment onto extremely constrained embedded devices.

One-Sentence Summary
QMD-4.0 is a deterministic, algebraic modeling pipeline that can fully describe a complex system using only a compact polynomial, a symmetry rule, and a lightweight state-transition kernel — all deployable on a float-only microcontroller.